Abstract
The integration of Artificial Intelligence into safety-critical systems presents a fundamental
challenge: stochastic AI models cannot be formally verified. This paper presents the TLA⁺
(Temporal Logic of Actions) formal specification and proof suite for ST-T1024, a deterministic
AI inference architecture. TLA⁺ provides a machine-checkable proof that the system is safe,
deterministic, bounded, auditable, and sovereign. For the first time, an AI architecture is not
merely designed for safety—it is provably safe. We demonstrate how TLA⁺ transforms
ST-T1024 from an open standard into a mathematical foundation for the deterministic age.
The specification establishes seven formal invariants—Safety, Determinism, Timing,
Isolation, Termination, Auditability, and Sovereignty—each verified through the TLC model
checker and amenable to TLAPS mechanical proof. This work has direct implications for
certification under ISO 26262, IEC 61508, and DO-178C, as the TLA⁺ Validation Test Suite
now enables tool qualification for safety-critical software development. The paper concludes
that formal verification replaces statistical confidence with absolute certainty, establishing a
new paradigm for certifiable AI.
