1. Introduction: The Verification Gap in AI
1.1 The Problem
For decades, the software industry has relied on testing to build confidence in systems. For
safety-critical applications—automotive, aerospace, medical devices, industrial
control—testing alone is insuficient. Standards such as ISO 26262, IEC 61508, and
DO-178C require evidence that the system behaves correctly under all possible conditions.Stochastic AI models present a fundamental challenge: they cannot be tested exhaustively,
and their behavior cannot be predicted with certainty. A transformer-based LLM with billions
of parameters has a state space that exceeds the number of atoms in the observable
universe. Testing can only sample a vanishingly small fraction of possible inputs.
This is not merely a practical limitation—it is a logical one. Stochastic systems, by their
nature, produce diferent outputs for the same inputs. They are non-deterministic.
Certification bodies cannot certify what they cannot predict.
1.2 The ST-T1024 Response
ST-T1024 was architected from the ground up to be deterministic. It replaces stochastic
transformer inference with a fully deterministic, Finite State Machine (FSM)-orchestrated,
ternary-quantized pipeline. Key properties include:
· Determinism: Identical inputs produce identical outputs
· Bounded Timing: Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET) ≤ 800 ns
· Isolation: Domain tiles are physically and logically independent
· Auditability: Every state transition is logged and traceable
· Sovereignty: Updates require cryptographic authorization (SK-1024)
But design intent is not proof. To achieve certification, these properties must be verified.
1.3 The Role of TLA⁺
TLA⁺ (Temporal Logic of Actions) is a formal specification language developed by Leslie
Lamport for modeling and verifying concurrent and distributed systems. It allows engineers
to express safety and liveness properties at a level of rigor that admits machine checking.
TLA⁺ provides two primary tools:
· TLC: An explicit-state model checker that can check both safety and liveness properties
· TLAPS: A proof system for mechanically checking proofs written in TLA⁺
For ST-T1024, TLA⁺ provides a machine-checkable proof that the architecture satisfies its
critical properties. This is not statistical confidence—it is mathematical certainty.
